\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Rationally isometric forms with different trace ratios}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{Take the rational [alternating four-cycle](fdip-000U),
with counting pairing #{g_0=I}, fixed #{m^2=1}, and opposite
vertices #{(0,2)} retained and #{(1,3)} eliminated.
For rational #{t>0}, the eigenvalues of its precision are
##{1,\quad3,\quad2t+1,\quad2t+3.}
Thus #{q_t(x)=x^TQ_tx} is positive, and
#{L_{\mathrm{rel}}=Q_t-I} is the earlier #{L_t}, with
nonnegative eigenvalues #{0,2,2t,2t+2}. Its trace is
#{4(t+1)>0}. These data lie in the full
[enriched observable domain](fdip-0041).}

\p{The [arithmetic domain calculation](fdip-000W) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\det Q_t&=3(2t+1)(2t+3),\\
R(t)&=\frac{t^2+t+1}{2(t+1)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
Here the determinant is also that of the half-polar pairing,
because #{g_0=I}. At the two positive rational parameters,
##{\begin{aligned}
\det Q_1&=45=5\cdot3^2,\\
R(1)&=\frac38,\\
\det Q_{29/2}&=2880=5\cdot24^2,\\
R(29/2)&=\frac{903}{1922}.
\end{aligned}}
For example, at #{t=29/2} the eigenvalues of
#{L_{\mathrm{rel}}} are #{0,2,29,31}; their sum is #{62}
and the sum of squares is #{1806}. Hence the second ratio is
#{1806/62^2=903/1922}. It differs from #{3/8}, since
#{8\cdot903=7224} and #{3\cdot1922=5766}.}

\p{In fact these forms are rationally isometric. The rational matrix
#{H/2} from \ref{fdip-000U} diagonalizes #{Q_t} by congruence as
#{\operatorname{diag}(1,3,2t+1,2t+3)}. The change of basis
##{\begin{aligned}
P&=\begin{pmatrix}1/2&5/2\\1/2&-3/2\end{pmatrix},
&\det P&=-2,\\
P^T\begin{pmatrix}3&0\\0&5\end{pmatrix}P
 &=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&30\end{pmatrix}
\end{aligned}}
therefore gives
##{q_1\simeq\langle1,3,3,5\rangle\simeq\langle1,3,2,30\rangle.}
At #{t=29/2}, rescaling the last coordinate by #{1/4} and then
permuting the last two coordinates gives
##{\begin{aligned}
q_{29/2}&\simeq\langle1,3,30,32\rangle\\
 &\simeq\langle1,3,30,2\rangle\simeq\langle1,3,2,30\rangle.
\end{aligned}}
All these changes of basis are invertible over #{\mathbb Q}. Thus
the [fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) already rules out recovery
of #{R} from the rational isometry class of #{q}.}

\p{Both signed discriminant classes are #{[5]}. Consequently
both even Clifford centers are #{\mathbb Q(\sqrt5)}, and both
have the same quadratic Galois character and the same primitive
Dirichlet representative #{\chi_5}, of conductor five.
In the [fixed uncompleted convention](fdip-0049), the values
#{L(2,\chi_5)} are identical. The
[fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) therefore rules out a
fixed function of this value recovering #{R} on this family.
The same conclusion holds for any fixed character-only
collection of L-values. It uses equality of the character,
not a numerical approximation to an L-value.}

\p{The loss already occurs when the supplied reference pairing is
forgotten. The exhibited quadratic isometry cannot preserve that
pairing: if it did, the relative precisions would be conjugate and
the trace ratios would agree. Hence no invariant determined solely
by the quadratic-isometry class, including an invariant of its
Clifford algebra or center, recovers #{R} on this family. Arithmetic
data retaining the reference pairing and mass are not excluded.
No physical QFT parameter has been identified with #{R}.}
